{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>The sons of Judah.</b> This passage alludes to the events that befell the people of Israel, and He chose to allude through Judah for the reason itself that they of blessed memory stated (Genesis Rabbah 98:6): why are Israel called by the name of Judah? The saying <b>the sons of Judah</b> — these are the descendants, which are subsumed under the name sons as is known. And the saying <b>Er and Onan</b> alludes to the First Temple and the Second Temple: Er corresponds to the First Temple, after the manner of the verse (Song of Songs 5:2): ״אֲנִי יְשֵׁנָה וְלִבִּי עֵר״ — for the Holy One, blessed be He, was awake in great providence with us in the Temple. Onan is the Second Temple, and it called him Onan in the language of אונאה (oppression/cheating), for it lacked the essential things of the Temple." "And the saying <b>and Er and Onan died</b> is the destruction of the Temples, for the departure of the Shekhinah from them is called death, since the soul — which is the Shekhinah — flew away, and the Temple remained dead through sins and filled with the impurity of the kelipah placed in it, as is known. Also you will find that the sin through which Er died is the very sin that caused the destruction: for they of blessed memory said (Shabbat 62b) that they would stain their beds, and that is the deed of Er, as it is written (Genesis 38:9): ״וְשִׁחֵת אַרְצָה״. And the sin of Onan is the sin that caused the destruction of the Second Temple, which was baseless hatred (Yoma 9b), and likewise Onan, concerning whom it is written (ibid.): ״לְבִלְתִּי נְתָן זֶרַע לְאָחִיו״ — also in the language of אונאה, for they would wrong one another." "And the saying <b>in the land of Canaan</b>: He marked the place of the Temple, for it is in the land of Canaan, and there is its death — that it returned to enter under the dominion of Samael, who is called Canaan. Further, it alludes to one general cause, namely that Israel did not dispossess the Canaanite and they remained dwelling in it, and this caused them to turn aside from the way of discipline as is known, and this is what He alluded to in the phrase ״in the land of Canaan״." "And the saying <b>and the sons of Judah were</b> alludes to the suffering that came upon them after the destruction of the Temple — woe, the sons of Judah were in exile at the hand of their enemies. Happy is he who has not tasted the dominion of the enemy. And you will find that for none of the tribes did the verse use this expression of ״וַיִּהְיוּ״ (and they were) except here and in Benjamin, as the reason there is known." "And the saying <b>after their families</b>: for all the families of the house of Israel, in every place they are, all who are called by the name of Judah — woe, they were." "And the verse's saying <b>of Shelah</b> intends to allude to the savior and great one who will come to redeem from the final exile, who is called Shelah. And although Jacob called him Shiloh, the name does not change on this account — go out and learn from the families listed how much change there is in names. Or perhaps it alludes, by the juxtaposition of the phrase <b>of Shelah</b> with the phrase <b>וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יְהוּדָה</b> ('and the sons of Judah were'), that Israel need to feel pain on his account and hope for his salvation, as the Sages said in the story of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, when the Messiah asked him whether Israel recognize his suffering and are pained by it, etc., up to there. And this is in accordance with the words of the Zohar (Beshalach 55a) in interpreting the verse ״קוֹל צֹפַיִךְ נָשְׂאוּ קוֹל״ etc. (Isaiah 52:8). And this is what the verse ״וַיִּהְיוּ״ means: they shall be ('יִהְיוּ') with a vav, which is suffering, ״לְמִשְׁפְּחוֹתָם״ ('for their families') — for the sake of Shelah; and this is what it says ״לְשֵׁלָה״ etc. And in this sense <b>מִשְׁפַּחַת הַשֵּׁלָנִי</b> ('the Shelanite family') means: the generation in which the son of David comes, and they will be called by his name — the generation of the Messiah." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes to what we may examine in its saying <b>הַשֵּׁלָנִי</b> — for there is no letter nun in the standard formation of the family name, and this is not the usual practice, to add a new letter to the family name besides hei and yod, as stated in all the preceding ones: ״חֲנוֹךְ מִשְׁפַּחַת הַחֲנוֹכִי״ etc. Rather, it alludes to what the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Shabbat 32b) regarding the verse ״וְהֶחֱזִיקוּ״ etc. ״בִּכְנַף אִישׁ יְהוּדִי״ etc. — see their words there. And this is what is alluded to in the phrase <b>הַשֵּׁלָנִי</b>: meaning that all the nations will say to every one of Israel, ״I am his\" (שֶׁלּוֹ אֲנִי)." "And the verse's saying <b>of Perez, the Perezite family</b> alludes, in the manner of their saying (Sanhedrin 20a), that the king breaks through a fence to make a path for himself. The intention is that when the one who comes from Perez arrives, all Israel will be of the Perezite family — kings. And it is of that time that the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Bava Metzia 113a): all Israel are children of kings; and so the Prophet said (Isaiah 49:7): ״מְלָכִים יִרְאוּ וְקָמוּ״ etc. It also alludes to the great breach that will be made among the nations without end." "And the verse's saying <b>of Zerah, the Zerahite family</b> alludes to what Scripture said (Isaiah 60:2): ״הִנֵּה הַחֹשֶׁךְ״ etc. ״וְעָלַיִךְ יִזְרַח ה׳״, and it is written ״וְהָיָה לָךְ ה׳ לְאוֹר עוֹלָם״. And this is what it says — ״מִשְׁפַּחַת הַזַּרְחִי״: that YHWH will shine upon us." "<b>Or</b> the section alludes to three matters that will come to pass at the time of the end: (1) the coming of Messiah son of Ephraim, as they said, whose memory is for blessing (Sukkah 52b); (2) the revelation of Messiah son of David; (3) when YHWH will reign over us. Corresponding to the coming of Messiah son of Ephraim, it says <b>Shelah</b>, alluding that the attribute of judgment will strike him and he will die for the iniquities of Israel — and this is alluded to in the word Shelah, in the manner of its saying (II Samuel 6:7): ״וַיַּכֵּהוּ שָׁם עַל הַשַּׁל״. And corresponding to Messiah son of David, it says <b>of Perez</b> etc., for this one will make a breach among the nations and avenge the blood of the righteous. And corresponding to the eternal kingdom, it says <b>of Zerah</b> etc. — an allusion to the glory of YHWH that will shine upon us." "And the verse's saying <b>וַיִּהְיוּ</b> the sons of Peretz — it uses a term of distress (צַעַר) to allude to the troubles that will come with the arrival of the Redeemer, as they said in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 98a): ״יֵיתֵי וְלֹא אֶחְמֵינֵיהּ״ — ״May he come but I may not see him״ — because of the abundance of distress from the birth pangs of Mashiach. And the verse's saying <b>בְּנֵי פֶרֶץ</b> etc. — meaning, the generations that will emerge from the coming of the Redeemer, alluded to in Peretz, are two things: (1) that HaShem will build the courtyard of the royal palace, which is the Temple that HaShem made and His hands established, and Israel will be the princes of His palace — this is what the verse says: <b>לְחֶצְרוֹן מִשְׁפַּחַת הַחֶצְרוֹנִי</b> — understand this. (2) <b>לְחָמוּל מִשְׁפַּחַת הֶחָמוּלִי</b> — that He will have compassion upon us with every branch of compassion." "And the verse's saying <b>אֵלֶּה מִשְׁפְּחוֹת יְהוּדָה</b> — meaning, what will befall them from the events, both the bad and the good. And the verse says ״לִפְקוּדֵיהֶם״ — meaning, when they are visited, there is a visitation for evil and there is a visitation for good, as stated in their words of blessed memory (Zohar Chadash 1:160a), and two kinds of visitations are stated in the verse." "And the verse's saying <b>שִׁשָּׁה וְשִׁבְעִים אֶלֶף וַחֲמֵשׁ מֵאוֹת</b> — perhaps HaShem alluded by this when the end of the good visitation recorded nearby will be, when the glory of HaShem will shine upon us. And He said: until all the exiles pass — the exile of Egypt, the exile of Babylon, and the exile of Edom. Corresponding to the exile of Egypt He said <b>שִׁשָּׁה</b>, alluding to the exile of Egypt, which lasted six generations, as it is written (Genesis 15:16): ״וְדוֹר רְבִיעִי יָשׁוּבוּ הֵנָּה״ — and see what I wrote there, that three generations endured exile besides Isaac and Jacob and Judah — behold six, for the beginning of the exile was from when Isaac was born. And corresponding to the exile of Babylon He said <b>וְשִׁבְעִים</b>, as it is written (Jeremiah 29:10): until the completion of Babylon, seventy years. And corresponding to the exile of Edom, in which we still are, He said <b>אֶלֶף וַחֲמֵשׁ מֵאוֹת</b> — and if Israel had merited, they would have been counted at 172 years remaining from the fourth thousand; but since they did not merit, the count begins from the fifth thousand and five hundred from the sixth thousand. And we hope that this is the end of our exiles, and all the recorded prophecies will be fulfilled, and the sparks of the revelation of redemption will begin to flash at the start of the five hundred years that come in peace."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {6 "The allusion turns on the nun in הַשֵּׁלָנִי producing a phrase read as שֶׁלּוֹ אֲנִי; the wordplay is visible only in Hebrew so it is transliterated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:34.136022647Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}